To force a text field to display text in columns using Adobe JavaScript, you can use the `setColumns` method of the `TextField` class. This method allows you to specify the number of columns you want the text field to display.

Here's an example of how you can use it:

```javascript
// Assuming you have a text field with the instance name "myTextField"
var myTextField = this.myTextField;

// Set the number of columns to 2
myTextField.setColumns(2);
```

In this example, the text field will display the text in 2 columns. You can change the number to any positive integer to display the text in the desired number of columns.

Make sure to run this code in the Adobe Flash/Animate environment, as it is specific to ActionScript, which is used for scripting in Adobe products.

